00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:23.060
Thank you. Your next question comes from the line of Michael Leuchten from Jefferies. Please go ahead.

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:29.680
Thank you for taking the follow-up. A question from Moco, please. The gross margin is pressured by

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:32.760
negative mixed effect, obviously. That is partly driven by

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:33.660
the genetic side of it. So, I'm going to ask you a question. So, I'm going to ask you a question

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:40.480
and partly by the new products having payaways. Is there a scale factor in here that we should

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:44.000
take into consideration as we think about the first half, second half of the year,

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:46.820
or is the gross margin just plain and simple, mixed driven?

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:54.420
Yeah. So, I think there is, as you rightly said, I think there's two things at play here. If we

00:00:54.420 --> 00:01:02.140
look at Q1, the overall decrease in the margin is 4.1%. Three percentage points of that comes from

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:03.580
R&D.

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:10.480
And a percentage point comes from the GX impact on gross margin. Now, GX impact on gross margin,

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:18.920
pretty much, I think if we take 2025 as a base, pretty much H2 base is what you guys should model

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:25.200
going into this year, and that should be the base. And as far as R&D is concerned, I think we

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:30.940
have to take into account not just the avidity deal that we did, but we also did three other

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:33.460
pretty significant deals in terminal.

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:48.500
So, in Q1, all of these three deals plus one month of avidity is incremental, and going forward,

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:54.940
I think part of it is already in the base. So, the incremental R&D spend would start to run off.

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:59.800
Thank you. Next question.

